<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bad Urach</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Urach</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bad Urach is a spa town on the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Jura</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the seat of the counts of Urach and later passed to Württemberg; from 1442 it was the residence of one of the two lines into which the county was divided, until reunification in 1482. Its castle and late Gothic buildings date largely from that period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bad-urach.jpg" alt="Bad Urach" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ssch · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BadUrachMarktplatz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
